<p>			    County Agent Comments</p>
<p>&quot;Hay production continues to be the main farming activity. Good weather conditions have helped the cotton and sweet potatoes to make up for lost time. Insect pressure has been light to this point.&quot;</p>
<p>					-- Ben Harlow, Calhoun County</p>
<p>&quot;The rain this weekend should have helped all crops, particularly the hay, pastures, and soybeans. No major problems with insects or disease reported. Silage harvesting will begin when the rain stops and ground conditions are favorable. &quot;</p>
<p>			     -- Dalton G. Garner, Prentiss County</p>
<p>&quot;Soybeans in the northern part of the county desperately need a good general rain. Producers are also waiting on a rain to control weeds in fields.&quot;</p>
<p>				   -- Otis L. Davis, Madison County</p>
<p>&quot;Boll weevil eradication got underway with no problems reported. No insect problems in cotton, except several hot spots of weevils. Minimal bollworm and budworm activity.&quot;</p>
<p>				    -- Fred W. Rose, Webster County</p>
